  "answer_text": "There are **three ways** to change the color of a `<span>` on mouse over in HTML. Here they are, from simplest to most flexible:\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 1: CSS `:hover` Pseudo-class (Recommended)\n\nThis is the cleanest and most modern approach.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <style>\n      span:hover {\n        color: red;\n      }\n    </style>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <span>Hover over me!</span>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nIf you know enough CSS to identify an element by its tag, id (`#`), or class (`.`), you can add the `:hover` selector to make the changes only appear when the user has moused over an element.\n\nYou can also target a specific `<span>` using a class or ID:\n\n```css\n/* By class */\n.my-span:hover {\n  color: blue;\n}\n\n/* By ID */\n#my-span:hover {\n  color: green;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 2: Inline JavaScript with `onmouseover` / `onmouseout`\n\nYou can use inline event handlers directly on the HTML element:\n\n```html\n<span\n  onmouseover=\"this.style.color='red';\"\n  onmouseout=\"this.style.color='black';\"\n>\n  Hover over me!\n</span>\n```\n\nThe JavaScript code is attached inside quotes to an HTML tag using event handlers like `onmouseover` and `onmouseout`. Inside those quotes, use the JavaScript keyword `this` to refer automatically to whatever HTML element the user is interacting with.\n\nAlso note that hyphenated CSS attributes like `background-color` must be converted to camelCase JavaScript ones like `backgroundColor`.\n\n> \u26a0\ufe0f Adding JavaScript inside HTML tags (\"inline\") is inflexible, time-consuming, and generally not recommended.\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 3: External JavaScript with Event Listeners (Best Practice)\n\nThis is the most flexible and scalable approach for larger projects:\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head></head>\n  <body>\n    <span id=\"my-span\">Hover over me!</span>\n\n    <script>\n      const span = document.getElementById(\"my-span\");\n\n      span.addEventListener(\"mouseover\", function () {\n        this.style.color = \"red\";\n      });\n\n      span.addEventListener(\"mouseout\", function () {\n        this.style.color = \"black\"; // Revert color on mouse out\n      });\n    </script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nThe `onmouseover` event is triggered when the user hovers over an element with the mouse. To restore the color after removing the mouse from the element, the `mouseout` event is also used, which fires when the mouse pointer leaves the element.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udfc6 Summary\n\n| Method | Ease | Separation of Concerns | Recommended |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CSS `:hover` | \u2b50 Easiest | \u2705 Best | \u2705 Yes |\n| Inline JS | \u2b50\u2b50 Easy | \u274c Poor | \u26a0\ufe0f Avoid |\n| JS Event Listeners | \u2b50\u2b50\u2b50 Moderate | \u2705 Good | \u2705 Yes |\n\n**The CSS `:hover` method is generally the best choice** for simply changing a color on mouse over, as it requires the least code and keeps your styling separate from your HTML and JavaScript.",
